44	{Nam \MakeLowercase{\textit{et al.}}: AoI-Aware V2I Uplink Scheduling via DRL}
45	
46	\maketitle
47	
48	% ==============================================================================
49	% ABSTRACT
50	% ==============================================================================
51	\begin{abstract}
52	In vehicle-to-infrastructure (V2I) networks, roadside units (RSUs) maintain real-time awareness of surrounding vehicles by receiving periodic mobility updates.
53	However, indiscriminate update scheduling wastes uplink resources and exacerbates channel congestion, while infrequent updates degrade the freshness of information.
54	This paper formulates the V2I uplink scheduling problem as a semi-Markov decision process (SMDP) that jointly optimizes the update interval, transmit power, and subchannel selection for each vehicle.
55	We propose a deep reinforcement learning (DRL) framework with a hybrid action space that combines continuous control of the update interval $\Delta$ and transmit power $p$ with discrete subchannel selection.
56	The reward function penalizes estimation error, power consumption, channel congestion, and redundant updates, where the weighting coefficients are jointly optimized via Optuna hyperparameter search.
57	We build a genuine simulation environment using SUMO microscopic traffic simulator integrated with a 5.9\,GHz Rayleigh fading channel model, and evaluate the proposed approach against nine baseline methods including three foundational RL algorithms and six recent AoI-aware scheduling schemes.
58	Simulation results demonstrate that the proposed method achieves a favorable trade-off between information freshness and resource efficiency under varying traffic densities.
59	\end{abstract}
60	
61	\begin{IEEEkeywords}
62	Age of information, vehicle-to-infrastructure, uplink scheduling, deep reinforcement learning, hybrid action space, semi-Markov decision process.
63	\end{IEEEkeywords}
64	
65	% ==============================================================================
66	% I. INTRODUCTION
67	% ==============================================================================
68	\section{Introduction}
69	\IEEEPARstart{T}{he} rapid advent of Connected and Autonomous Vehicles (CAVs) and Cooperative Intelligent Transportation Systems (C-ITS) is fundamentally transforming modern vehicular networks~\cite{al-khasawneh2026uav}. For safety-critical applications such as cooperative collision avoidance, platooning, and intelligent intersection management, it is imperative for the infrastructure to maintain highly accurate and real-time mobility data—such as the position, velocity, and acceleration—of surrounding vehicles~\cite{zhang2025small, li2025next}. To guarantee the reliability of these advanced ITS services, vehicles must continuously exchange their dynamic state information with the infrastructure, making real-time data tracking a cornerstone of future vehicular networks.
70	
71	However, accommodating this massive influx of real-time mobility data poses severe challenges to existing communication architectures. While relying purely on Cellular Vehicle-to-Network (V2N) communications provides wide coverage, it incurs prohibitive monetary costs and suffers from backhaul latency bottlenecks~\cite{sun2025overcoming}. Consequently, managing local mobility information through Roadside Units (RSUs) via Vehicle-to-Infrastructure (V2I) communication is considered a practical and scalable alternative. Yet, even within the localized coverage of an RSU, if vehicles blindly broadcast their status at high, fixed frequencies (e.g., 10 Hz Cooperative Awareness Messages in ETSI standards), it inevitably leads to catastrophic wireless channel congestion and packet collisions~\cite{park2025adaptive}. Furthermore, such purely periodic transmissions recklessly drain the limited energy resources of vehicles, presenting a critical bottleneck for the sustainability of real-time V2I tracking~\cite{duan2026energy}.
72	
73	% --- Limitations of Prior Work ---
74	Existing AoI-aware scheduling approaches~\cite{mlika2022deep, qi2025deep, zhang2025drl} typically treat the update decision as a simple binary choice or optimize only a single dimension (e.g., update interval or power) in isolation.
75	Moreover, most prior works adopt simplified channel models that do not capture the stochastic nature of V2I uplink transmissions under Rayleigh fading and multi-user interference.
76	
77	% --- Our Approach ---
78	In this paper, we formulate the V2I uplink scheduling problem as a semi-Markov decision process (SMDP) and propose a deep reinforcement learning framework with a \emph{hybrid action space}.
79	The agent jointly decides three control variables for each vehicle: (i) a continuous update interval $\Delta \in [\Delta_{\min}, \Delta_{\max}]$, (ii) a continuous transmit power $p \in [p_{\min}, p_{\max}]$, and (iii) a discrete subchannel index $\mathrm{ch} \in \{0, 1, \ldots, C{-}1\}$.
80	The reward function incorporates four penalty terms---estimation error, power consumption, channel congestion, and redundant updates---each normalized to $[0, 1]$ and weighted by coefficients $w_1 \sim w_4$ that are optimized via Optuna hyperparameter search.
81	
82	% --- Contributions ---
83	The main contributions of this paper are summarized as follows:
84	\begin{itemize}
85	    \item We formulate the AoI-aware V2I uplink scheduling problem as an SMDP with a hybrid action space that jointly optimizes the update interval, transmit power, and subchannel selection.
86	    \item We design a four-term reward function with Min-Max normalization and Optuna-optimized weighting coefficients that captures the multi-dimensional trade-off between information freshness, resource consumption, and channel congestion.
87	    \item We implement a genuine simulation environment based on the SUMO microscopic traffic simulator with a physically accurate 5.9\,GHz Rayleigh fading channel model, and conduct rigorous evaluation against nine baseline methods.
88	    \item We introduce an Act/Rest dual-model hot-swap training architecture that enables continuous inference during background model updates, ensuring hardware feasibility for real-time deployment.
89	\end{itemize}
90	
91	The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec:related} reviews related work. Section~\ref{sec:system} presents the system model. Section~\ref{sec:problem} formulates the SMDP problem. Section~\ref{sec:proposed} describes the proposed DRL framework. Section~\ref{sec:evaluation} presents the simulation setup and results. Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes the paper.
92	
93	% ==============================================================================
94	% II. RELATED WORK
95	% ==============================================================================
96	\section{Related Work}
97	\label{sec:related}
98	
99	\subsection{Age of Information in Vehicular Networks}
100	The concept of AoI was originally introduced for status update systems~\cite{kaul2012real} and has since been extensively studied in vehicular networks.
101	
102	Mlika and Cherkaoui~\cite{mlika2022deep} applied DDPG to minimize AoI in cellular V2X communications, demonstrating the feasibility of DRL-based scheduling for vehicular scenarios.
103	Qi \emph{et~al.}~\cite{qi2025deep} proposed a SAC-based resource allocation scheme for RIS-aided IoV networks, incorporating AoI awareness into the reward design.
104	Arani \emph{et~al.}~\cite{arani2026resilient} developed a resilient AoI-aware optimization framework using DRL for intelligent transportation systems.
105	Zhang \emph{et~al.}~\cite{zhang2025drl} jointly optimized AoI and energy consumption in C-V2X enabled IoV using DRL.
106	
107	\subsection{Resource Allocation in V2X Networks}
108	Azizi \emph{et~al.}~\cite{azizi2024efficient} proposed a multi-agent RL mechanism for AoI-aware resource management in VLC-V2X networks.
109	Lin \emph{et~al.}~\cite{lin2025optimization} optimized spectrum resource allocation for vehicle platoons using DRL.
110	
111	\subsection{Hybrid Action Spaces in DRL}
112	While prior works predominantly adopt either fully continuous or fully discrete action spaces, our work jointly handles continuous (interval, power) and discrete (subchannel) variables through a hybrid action space architecture.
113	
114	% ==============================================================================
115	% III. SYSTEM MODEL
116	% ==============================================================================
117	\section{System Model}
118	\label{sec:system}
119	
120	\subsection{Network Architecture}
121	We consider a V2I network where RSUs are deployed at intersections of an urban grid road network.
122	Each RSU has a communication range of $R_{\mathrm{RSU}} = 300$\,m operating at a carrier frequency of 5.9\,GHz, consistent with the C-V2X sidelink specification.
123	The uplink bandwidth of 20\,MHz is divided into $C = 4$ subchannels.
124	Vehicles enter and exit the RSU's coverage area as they traverse the road network, which is simulated using the SUMO microscopic traffic simulator with a step resolution of 0.1\,s.
125	
126	\subsection{RSU Awareness Table}
127	Each RSU maintains an awareness table that stores, for each registered vehicle $i$, the last received mobility state $(\mathbf{x}_i, \mathbf{v}_i, \tau_i)$, where $\mathbf{x}_i$ is the position, $\mathbf{v}_i$ is the velocity, and $\tau_i$ is the timestamp of the last successful update.
128	Between updates, the RSU extrapolates the vehicle's position using a constant-velocity model:
129	\begin{equation}
130	    \hat{\mathbf{x}}_i(t) = \mathbf{x}_i + \mathbf{v}_i \cdot (t - \tau_i).
131	    \label{eq:extrapolation}
132	\end{equation}
133	
134	The estimation error at time $t$ is defined as:
135	\begin{equation}
136	    e_i(t) = \| \hat{\mathbf{x}}_i(t) - \mathbf{x}_i^{\mathrm{true}}(t) \|_2,
137	    \label{eq:error}
138	\end{equation}
139	where $\mathbf{x}_i^{\mathrm{true}}(t)$ is the true position obtained from the SUMO simulator.
140	
141	\subsection{Wireless Channel Model}
142	The uplink transmission from vehicle $i$ to the RSU follows a path loss model with Rayleigh fading.
143	The received power at distance $d_i$ is:
144	\begin{equation}
145	    P_{\mathrm{rx},i} = P_{\mathrm{tx},i} - \mathrm{PL}(d_i),
146	\end{equation}
147	where the path loss is given by:
148	\begin{equation}
149	    \mathrm{PL}(d) = 20\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{4\pi f_c}{c}\right) + 10 \alpha \log_{10}(d) \quad [\mathrm{dB}],
150	\end{equation}
151	with carrier frequency $f_c = 5.9$\,GHz and path loss exponent $\alpha = 2.3$.
152	
153	For vehicles transmitting on the same subchannel, the success probability under Rayleigh fading is given by the closed-form expression:
154	\begin{equation}
155	    P_{\mathrm{succ},i} = \exp\!\left(-\frac{\gamma_{\mathrm{th}} N_0}{S_i}\right) \prod_{k \neq i} \frac{1}{1 + \gamma_{\mathrm{th}} I_k / S_i},
156	    \label{eq:sinr}
157	\end{equation}
158	where $\gamma_{\mathrm{th}}$ is the SINR threshold, $N_0$ is the noise power, $S_i$ is the received signal power from vehicle $i$, and $I_k$ is the interference from co-channel vehicle $k$.
159	
160	\subsection{Vehicle Events}
161	A vehicle's lifecycle within an RSU cell consists of three events:
162	\begin{itemize}
163	    \item \textbf{E1 (Entry)}: Vehicle enters the RSU's coverage, performs initial registration, and receives its first update schedule.
164	    \item \textbf{E2 (Scheduled Update)}: At the scheduled time, the vehicle transmits its current state. The transmission succeeds or fails according to~\eqref{eq:sinr}.
165	    \item \textbf{E3 (Exit)}: Vehicle leaves the coverage area and is deregistered.
166	\end{itemize}
167	
168	% ==============================================================================
169	% IV. PROBLEM FORMULATION
170	% ==============================================================================
171	\section{Problem Formulation}
172	\label{sec:problem}
173	
174	\subsection{Semi-Markov Decision Process}
175	We model the scheduling problem as an SMDP, where the decision epochs are not uniformly spaced but occur at each vehicle's scheduled update time.
176	
177	\subsubsection{State Space}
178	The state observation for vehicle $i$ at decision epoch $t$ is an 18-dimensional vector:
179	\begin{equation}
180	    \mathbf{s}_i(t) = \left[ v_{\mathrm{pos}}, d_{\mathrm{rsu}}, v_{\mathrm{heading}}, v_{\mathrm{vel}}, \mathrm{tls}_{\mathrm{state}}, \mathrm{tls}_{\mathrm{dist}}, n_{\mathrm{queue}}, n_{\mathrm{active}}, \ldots \right]^{\!\top}\!,
181	\end{equation}
182	which includes:
183	\begin{itemize}
184	    \item Vehicle kinematics: position $v_{\mathrm{pos}}$, distance to RSU $d_{\mathrm{rsu}}$, heading $v_{\mathrm{heading}}$ (approaching/leaving), velocity $v_{\mathrm{vel}}$.
185	    \item Traffic signal context: signal state $\mathrm{tls}_{\mathrm{state}}$ (R/Y/G), distance to stop line $\mathrm{tls}_{\mathrm{dist}}$.
186	    \item Congestion indicators: number of queued vehicles ahead $n_{\mathrm{queue}}$, number of active vehicles in range $n_{\mathrm{active}}$.
187	    \item Historical context: information from previously updated neighboring vehicles stored in the RSU table.
188	\end{itemize}
189	
190	\subsubsection{Action Space}
191	The hybrid action for vehicle $i$ is a tuple:
192	\begin{equation}
193	    \mathbf{a}_i = (\Delta_i,\; p_i,\; \mathrm{ch}_i),
194	\end{equation}
195	where:
196	\begin{itemize}
197	    \item $\Delta_i \in [\Delta_{\min}, \Delta_{\max}]$: next update interval (continuous). The upper bound $\Delta_{\max}$ is set to the maximum red-phase duration of traffic lights in the simulation.
198	    \item $p_i \in [p_{\min}, p_{\max}] = [10, 23]$\,dBm: transmit power (continuous), bounded by the 3GPP power class~3 UE maximum.
199	    \item $\mathrm{ch}_i \in \{0, 1, \ldots, C{-}1\}$: subchannel index (discrete).
200	\end{itemize}
201	
202	\subsubsection{Reward Function}
203	The reward is a weighted sum of four normalized penalty terms:
204	\begin{equation}
205	    R_t = -\!\left( w_1 \cdot \overline{e_t^2} + w_2 \cdot \overline{P_{\mathrm{tx}}} + w_3 \cdot \overline{C_{\mathrm{freq}}} + w_4 \cdot \mathbb{I}_{\mathrm{redundant}} \right),
206	    \label{eq:reward}
207	\end{equation}
208	where the overline denotes Min-Max normalization to $[0,1]$:
209	\begin{itemize}
210	    \item $\overline{e_t^2}$: normalized squared estimation error between the RSU's extrapolated and true vehicle positions.
211	    \item $\overline{P_{\mathrm{tx}}} = (p - p_{\min}) / (p_{\max} - p_{\min})$: normalized transmit power penalty.
212	    \item $\overline{C_{\mathrm{freq}}}$: normalized channel congestion indicator based on the channel busy ratio (CBR) and SINR collision rate.
213	    \item $\mathbb{I}_{\mathrm{redundant}} \in \{0, 1\}$: binary indicator that imposes a penalty when the vehicle's physical state has not changed since the last update (e.g., a stopped vehicle).
214	\end{itemize}
215	
216	The weighting coefficients $w_1, w_2, w_3, w_4$ are not heuristically fixed but are included in the Optuna hyperparameter search space, allowing the agent to discover the optimal balance across penalty terms.
217	
218	\subsection{SMDP Discounting}
219	Since the time between successive actions for a given vehicle varies, we employ the SMDP discount factor $\gamma^{\Delta_i}$, which accounts for the variable sojourn time in the replay buffer.
220	
221	% ==============================================================================
222	% V. PROPOSED METHOD
223	% ==============================================================================
224	\section{Proposed DRL Framework}
225	\label{sec:proposed}
226	
227	\subsection{Hybrid Action Architecture}
228	The proposed DRL agent outputs the hybrid action $\mathbf{a}_i = (\Delta_i, p_i, \mathrm{ch}_i)$ through a multi-head neural network.
229	Two continuous heads produce $\Delta_i$ and $p_i$ via tanh activation with subsequent scaling, while a discrete head selects $\mathrm{ch}_i$ via categorical sampling.
230	
231	\subsection{Act/Rest Dual-Model Hot-Swap}
232	To ensure real-time inference feasibility, we adopt an Act/Rest dual-model architecture:
233	\begin{itemize}
234	    \item \textbf{Act mode}: The active model serves real-time scheduling decisions while collecting transitions into a replay buffer.
235	    \item \textbf{Rest mode}: A background model trains on the accumulated transitions using a separate hardware resource.
236	    \item \textbf{Hot-swap}: When the Rest model's training is complete, it seamlessly replaces the Act model with zero downtime, and the cycle repeats.
237	\end{itemize}
238	This architecture ensures that the training workload does not interfere with inference latency, which is critical for time-sensitive V2I scheduling.
239	
240	\subsection{Hyperparameter Optimization}
241	We employ Optuna for hyperparameter optimization (HPO), searching over the reward weights $w_1 \sim w_4$ and model-specific hyperparameters (learning rate, discount factor, etc.).
242	Each trial trains the agent and evaluates it across multiple seeds and traffic densities.
243	
244	% ==============================================================================
245	% VI. PERFORMANCE EVALUATION
246	% ==============================================================================
247	\section{Performance Evaluation}
248	\label{sec:evaluation}
249	
250	\subsection{Simulation Setup}
251	
252	\subsubsection{Traffic Simulation}
253	We use SUMO (Simulation of Urban Mobility) v1.27 to generate realistic vehicular traffic on an urban grid network.
254	Table~\ref{tab:sim_params} summarizes the key simulation parameters.
255	
256	\begin{table}[!t]
257	\centering
258	\caption{Simulation Parameters}
259	\label{tab:sim_params}
260	\begin{tabular}{lc}
261	\toprule
262	\textbf{Parameter} & \textbf{Value} \\
263	\midrule
264	Carrier frequency & 5.9\,GHz \\
265	Bandwidth & 20\,MHz \\
266	Number of subchannels & 4 \\
267	RSU communication range & 300\,m \\
268	Transmit power range & [10, 23]\,dBm \\
269	Path loss exponent & 2.3 \\
270	SINR threshold & 0\,dB \\
271	SUMO step length & 0.1\,s \\
272	Episode length & 2000 steps (200\,s) \\
273	Number of episodes & 100 (200k steps total) \\
274	Vehicle density & 15--55\,veh/km \\
275	\bottomrule
276	\end{tabular}
277	\end{table}
278	
279	\subsubsection{Baseline Methods}
280	We compare the proposed method against nine baselines:
281	\begin{itemize}
282	    \item \textbf{Foundation RL models}: PPO~\cite{ppo}, SAC~\cite{sac}, TD3~\cite{td3}.
283	    \item \textbf{AoI-aware DRL schemes}: SAC-RIS~\cite{qi2025deep}, DDPG-CV2X~\cite{mlika2022deep}, DDPG-Resilient~\cite{arani2026resilient}, MARL-VLC~\cite{azizi2024efficient}, Platoon-DRL~\cite{lin2025optimization}, DRL-IoV~\cite{zhang2025drl}.
284	\end{itemize}
285	
286	All baselines are adapted to the same hybrid action space and trained on the identical SUMO environment for fair comparison.
287	
288	\subsection{Training Configuration}
289	Each model is trained for 200,000 environment steps (100 episodes $\times$ 2,000 steps).
290	Optuna HPO is used to optimize the reward weights and model hyperparameters over multiple trials.
291	Training progress is monitored via TensorBoard.
292	
293	\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}
294	We evaluate the following metrics across varying vehicle densities (15--55\,veh/km) and multiple random seeds:
295	\begin{itemize}
296	    \item Mean estimation error ($\overline{e^2}$).
297	    \item Uplink success rate.
298	    \item Mean AoI across all vehicles.
299	    \item Total energy consumption.
300	    \item Channel utilization efficiency.
301	\end{itemize}
302	
303	\subsection{Results and Discussion}
304	
305	% Placeholder for results
306	\textit{(Results to be added after the 200,000-step training runs are completed and analyzed.)}
307	
308	% ==============================================================================
309	% VII. CONCLUSION
310	% ==============================================================================
311	\section{Conclusion}
312	\label{sec:conclusion}
313	
314	In this paper, we proposed a DRL-based V2I uplink scheduling framework that jointly optimizes the update interval, transmit power, and subchannel selection through a hybrid action space.
315	The four-term reward function with Optuna-optimized weights captures the complex trade-off between information freshness, power efficiency, channel congestion, and update redundancy.
316	The Act/Rest dual-model hot-swap architecture ensures real-time inference feasibility.
317	Extensive simulations on a genuine SUMO traffic environment with a 5.9\,GHz Rayleigh fading channel model validate the effectiveness of the proposed approach.
318	
